eneral-election polls and the resolution of both parties' primaries could move this forecast substantially in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "North Carolina enters the 2028 presidential cycle as one of the closest battlegrounds in the country. The state has favored Republicans by narrow margins in recent presidential elections, and its partisan lean sits near even. With the incumbent president term-limited, neither party benefits from White House incumbency, leaving an open contest that begins essentially at the state's underlying balance.\n\nNo public general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, and neither party has settled on a nominee. That absence of race-specific evidence keeps the forecast anchored to fundamentals and carries wide uncertainty. Recent results suggest the eventual margin will likely be decided by low single digits, but the identity of the nominees and the national environment in 2028 could easily tip the state toward either party.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4508, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4639, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4659, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4636, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4637, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.4628, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4642, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.4667, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.4617, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.4598, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.4577, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.4622, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.4697, "margin": -1.0}